\title{Complete Verbatim W*-Algebra Discussion}
\author{User \& Assistant}
\date{\today}
\maketitle

\section*{Chat Transcript}

\subsection*{Initial Question}

\textbf{User:} Is the operator norm of a projector in a W* algebra always 1?

\subsection*{Response 1}

\textbf{Assistant:} Yes. For any non-zero projection $P $ in a W*-algebra: 
\begin{equation*}
\|P\| = 1 
\end{equation*}
This holds because:

\begin{itemize}
\item $P $ is idempotent ($P^2 = P $)

\item Its spectrum satisfies $\sigma(P) \subseteq \{0,1\} $

\item The spectral radius equals the norm: $r(P) = \|P\| = 1 $

\item The zero projection has norm 0
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Follow-up Questions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item Do all operators belonging to a C* algebra have a spectrum?

\item What elements of a W* algebra have a spectral resolution?

\item Can the measure be decomposed into independent discrete, continuous
and singular parts?

\item What are the spectral decompositions of infinite projectors in Type II
and Type III W* algebras?

\item Are the extreme points of these convex sets always projections?

\item What is the difference between a positive cone and convex set of
positive operators?

\item Is the norm of a bounded positive linear functional defined on a W*
algebra the sup of its values on the set of projections?

\item Is there a name for subspaces of W* algebras generated by a set of
projections?
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{Comprehensive Answers}

\subsubsection*{Spectrum in C*-Algebras}

For any operator $a $ in a C*-algebra $\mathcal{A} $: 
\begin{equation*}
\sigma(a) \neq \emptyset \quad \text{and compact in } \mathbb{C} 
\end{equation*}
Key points:

\begin{itemize}
\item In unital algebras: $\sigma(a) = \{\lambda \in \mathbb{C} \mid a -
\lambda 1 \text{ not invertible}\} $

\item In non-unital algebras: Use unitization $\widetilde{\mathcal{A}} $

\item Proof uses Gelfand-Mazur theorem and holomorphic functional calculus
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{Spectral Resolution}

Normal operators $T $ (where $T^*T = TT^* $) admit: 
\begin{equation*}
T = \int_{\sigma(T)} \lambda \, dE(\lambda) 
\end{equation*}
with projection-valued measure $E $. Special cases:

\begin{itemize}
\item Self-adjoint: $\sigma(T) \subset \mathbb{R} $

\item Unitary: $\sigma(T) \subset \{z \mid |z|=1\} $

\item Projections: $\sigma(P) \subseteq \{0,1\} $
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{Measure Decomposition}

The spectral measure $E $ decomposes as: 
\begin{equation*}
E = E_d + E_{ac} + E_{sc} 
\end{equation*}
where:

\begin{itemize}
\item $E_d $: Discrete (atomic) part

\item $E_{ac} $: Absolutely continuous w.r.t. Lebesgue measure

\item $E_{sc} $: Singular continuous (e.g., Cantor-type measures)
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{Type II/III Projectors}

For infinite projections $P $ in Type II/III algebras: 
\begin{equation*}
P = P_d, \quad P_{ac} = 0, \quad P_{sc} = 0 
\end{equation*}
Key differences from Type I:

\begin{itemize}
\item No minimal projections exist

\item All non-zero projections in Type III are equivalent

\item Still have purely atomic spectral measures
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{Extreme Points}

The extreme points of $\{a \in \mathcal{M}_+ \mid \|a\| \leq 1\} $ are
exactly the projections: 
\begin{equation*}
\text{Ext}(\mathcal{M}_+^1) = \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{M}) 
\end{equation*}
Proof uses:

\begin{itemize}
\item Idempotence $P^2 = P $

\item Spectral theory showing $\sigma(P) \subseteq \{0,1\} $

\item Convexity arguments
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{Positive Cone vs. Convex Set}

\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Positive Cone} $\mathcal{M}_+ $ & \textbf{Convex Set} $\mathcal{M}%
_+^1 $ \\ \hline
All $a \geq 0 $ & $\{a \geq 0 \mid \|a\| \leq 1\} $ \\ 
Closed under addition & Closed under convex combinations \\ 
Not convex itself & Convex body \\ 
Extreme point: 0 & Extreme points: projections \\ \hline
\end{tabular}

\subsubsection*{Functional Norms}

For positive linear functionals $\phi $: 
\begin{equation*}
\|\phi\| = \sup_{p \in \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{M})} \phi(p) = \phi(1) 
\end{equation*}
Holds because:

\begin{itemize}
\item Projections are extreme points

\item Norm is achieved at the identity
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{Projection-Generated Subspaces}

Names include:

\begin{itemize}
\item 
\lbrack leftmargin=*]

\item Projection lattices (if closed under $\vee, \wedge $)

\item CSL algebras (commutative subspace lattices)

\item Operator systems (general case)

\item Free product algebras (in free probability)
\end{itemize}

